      {
        "id": 21281,
        "name": "Citizen Jodie",
        "username": "Citizen_Jodie",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/citizen_jodie/{size}/2316_2.png",
        "created_at": "2011-08-17T05:53:53.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"&quot;&quot;evandan457&quot;&quot;\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n \"\"evandan457\"\":</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I agree. I’m no businessman like my father, but if you’re at a place where you’re trying to sell your product, it helps get excited about it. You guys made the game fun and exciting. Whereas–I won’t mention names–some other booths didn’t even try to seem like their game was exciting.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I noticed this at Gen Con too.  So many booths, I’m sure, had fine games, and I would have been more-than-willing to sit down and play a demo of any game someone had approached me to play.  I literally only walked past one other booth that asked me if I wanted to play their game.  Unfortunately that other booth kept telling me their game was like a much better known game which made me wonder, as I left their booth, why I wouldn’t just go buy the game their game is like.</p>",
        "post_number": 22,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T01:55:22.694Z",
